<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Young Italy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1853">1853</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as La Giovine Italia, Giovine Italia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> (La Giovine Italia) was an Italian political movement founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a> in Marseille in July 1831, while he was in exile. It aimed to create a united Italian republic through a general insurrection in the Italian reactionary states and the lands occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, and had about 60,000 members around 1833. Its revolts in Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> in 1833 and 1834 and later risings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, Tuscany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> failed, and many members were arrested and executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> joined it around 1833; it was affiliated with Mazzini's Giovine Europa, and was effectively ended after a last failed revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1853.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/young-italy.jpg" alt="Young Italy" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self-created · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_Giovine_Italia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
